Marangoni self-propelled capsules in a maze: pollutants ‘sense and act’ in complex channel environments†
Guanjia Zhao and Martin Pumera*
Environmental remediation is a highly pressing issue in society. Here we demonstrate that autonomous self-propelled millimeter sized capsules can sense the presence of pollutants, mark sites for visible identification and remove the contamination, while navigating in a complex environment of interconnected channels, the maze. Such long-range self-powered capsules propelled by the Marangoni effect are capable of releasing chemicals to alter the pH and induce aggregation during pollutant flocculation at a faster rate than convection or diffusion. These devices are foreseen to have real-world environmental applications in the near future.
Introduction
Currently, self-propelled autonomous devices on the nano, micro and macro-scale are very important research areas in materials science. They have been proposed to perform biomedical tasks, such as microsurgery and drug delivery,1,2 natural resources discovery3 and environmental remediation.4–6 The propulsion mechanisms of these devices, on the submillimeter (nano and micro) scale, use mechanisms of self-diffusiophoresis,7 self-electrophoresis8,9 and bubblepropulsion10 predominantly. Contrastingly, at the macro (millimeter and above) level, the dominant propulsion mechanism has been relying on the differences in the induced surface tension of the chemical environment, a phenomena otherwise termed as the Marangoni effect. As such, Marangoni effect propelled devices do not require external power sources and can travel over large distances, surpassing several tens of meters. Therefore, such self-powered machines are extremely attractive for environmental sensing and remediation. There have been reports on the environmental clean-up of oil spills with millimeter sized Marangoni effect powered devices5 as well as microengines.11 Recently, micromotor devices producing active oxygen species by the Fenton reaction were used to spread an active decontaminant in a bulk
solution to remove the polluting dye.12 In another work, microjet engines were used in a similar manner to stir the solution and enhance the rate of chemical reaction.13 However, to date, there has been no report on utilizing the autonomous motion of artificial motors in complex channel environments with regards to sensing applications and pollutant removal. A complex interconnected branching channel environment, referred to as the maze, offers the capability to test the interaction of living and inorganic systems. A typical example of utilization of a maze is the “maze solving” problem: finding the (shortest) route from point A to point B (start-tofinish route). It has been demonstrated that the problem of finding the shortest path from point A to B in the maze can be solved by passing high voltage (several kV) through an evacuated microfluidics network (with channel width of 250 μm).14 The resulting glow discharge marked the shortest pathway. In a similar fashion, microfluidic systems were used to find the routes of least fluidic resistance through a maze. This method used a liquid pressure driven system in the microfluidics channel maze with channels of hundreds of μm in width.15 Alternatively, the maze can be used for investigate the chemotactic behaviour of organisms. The size of the maze path should be compatible with size of the investigated organisms. Millimeter sized channels (∼3 mm in width) were carved to test the capability of plasmodium of the slime mould (Physarum polycephalum) to find the shortest route towards food.16 In similar way, 1 mm wide channels were utilized to study the behavioural plasticity of the worm Caenorhabditis elegans. These worms were also able to employ chemotaxis and find the shortest route to the source of food through the maze.17 It was demonstrated at a sub-millimeter
